== Longing After Spiritual Attainments

XI. Another great and very distinguishing difference between gracious affections and others is that the higher gracious affections are raised, the more a spiritual appetite and longing of soul after spiritual attainments is increased. False affections, on the contrary, rest satisfied in themselves. #footnote[Truly there is no work of Christ that is right (says Mr. #emph[Shepard]) but it carries the soul to long for more of it. Par. Of the ten virgins, Part I. P. 136. And again: There is in true grace an infinite circle: a man receives by thirsting, and by receiving thirsts for more. But this is why the Spirit is not poured out abundantly on churches: because men shut it out by shutting themselves in and contenting themselves with their common graces and gifts (Matt 7:29). Examine whether it is not so. Ibid, p. 182. And on p. 210 he says: This I say: true grace, as it comforts, so it never fills, but puts an edge on the appetite: more of that grace, Lord! So #emph[Paul,] Phil 3:13, 14. So #emph[David:] out of my poverty I have given, and so on (1 Chr 29:3, 17, 18). It is a sure way never to be deceived in lighter strokes of the Spirit, to be thankful for any of it, but to be content with no measure of it. And this cuts the thread of difference between a superficial, lighter stroke of the Spirit and one that is sound.]

The more a true saint loves God with a gracious love, the more he desires to love him, and the more uneasy he is at his lack of love to him. The more he hates sin, the more he desires to hate it, and laments that he has so much love for it remaining. The more he mourns for sin, the more he longs to mourn for sin. The more his heart is broken, the more he desires it to be broken. The more he thirsts and longs after God and holiness, the more he longs to long, and to breathe out his very soul in longings after God. The kindling and raising of gracious affections is like kindling a flame: the higher it is raised, the more ardent it is, and the more it burns, the more vehemently it tends and seeks to burn. So the spiritual appetite after holiness and after an increase of holy affections is much more lively and keen in those who are eminent in holiness than in others; and more when grace and holy affections are in their liveliest exercise than at other times. It is as much the nature of one who is spiritually newborn to thirst after growth in holiness as it is the nature of a newborn baby to thirst after its mother’s breast; and the baby has the sharpest appetite when it is in the best health. 1 Pet 2:2, 3: “Like newborn babies, crave pure spiritual milk, so that by it you#super[+] may grow up in your#super[+] salvation, now that you#super[+] have tasted that the Lord is good.” The most the saints have in this world is only a taste, a foretaste of that future glory which is their proper fullness. It is only an earnest of their future inheritance in their hearts (2 Cor 1:22; and 5:5; and Eph 1:14). The most eminent saints in this state are only children compared with their future state, which is their proper state of maturity and perfection, as the apostle observes in 1 Cor 13:10, 11. The greatest eminence and perfection the saints reach in this world has no tendency to make them sated or to lessen their desires for more. On the contrary, it makes them more eager to press forward, as is evident from the apostle’s words in Phil 3:13, 14, 15: “Forgetting what is behind and straining toward what is ahead, I press on toward the goal. … All of us who are PERFECT should embrace this point of view.”

The reasons for it are these. The more holy affections persons have, the more they have of that spiritual taste I have spoken of elsewhere, by which they perceive the excellence and relish the divine sweetness of holiness. And the more grace they have while in this state of imperfection, the more they see their imperfection and emptiness, and their distance from what ought to be; and so the more they see their need of grace, as I showed at length before when speaking of the nature of evangelical humiliation. Besides, grace, as long as it is imperfect, is of a growing nature and in a growing state. And we see that it is so with all living things: while they are in a state of imperfection and in their growing state, their nature seeks growth, and all the more as they are healthier and more thriving. Therefore the cry of every true grace is like that cry of true faith in Mark 9:24: “I do believe; help my unbelief!” And the greater the spiritual manifestations and affections the true Christian has, the more earnest a beggar he becomes for grace and spiritual food, so that he may grow; and the more earnestly he pursues it in the use of proper means and efforts. For true and gracious longings after holiness are no idle, ineffectual desires.

But here some may object and say: How is this consistent with what everyone grants, that spiritual enjoyments are of a soul-satisfying nature?

I answer that it will appear not at all inconsistent with what has been said, if we consider in what way spiritual enjoyments are said to be of a soul-satisfying nature. Certainly they are not so in the sense of being so cloying that the man who has anything of them, though only in a very imperfect degree, desires no more. But spiritual enjoyments are of a soul-satisfying nature in the following respects. 1. In their kind and nature they are fully suited to the nature, capacity and need of the soul of man. So those who find them desire #emph[no other kind] of enjoyment. They sit down fully content with the kind of happiness they have, desiring no change, and no longer inclined to wander about, saying, “Who can show us the good?” The soul is never cloyed, never weary, but perpetually gives itself up, with all its powers, to this happiness. But this does not mean that those who have something of this happiness desire no more of the same. 2. They are satisfying also in this respect, that they answer the expectation of the appetite. When the appetite for anything is strong, the expectation is strong as a result. Appetite for a particular object implies expectation in its very nature. This expectation is not satisfied by worldly enjoyments: the man expected to gain a great increase of happiness, but he is disappointed. But it is not so with spiritual enjoyments; they fully answer and satisfy the expectation. 3. The gratification and pleasure of spiritual enjoyments is lasting. It is not so with worldly enjoyments. In a sense they satisfy particular appetites. But in being satisfied the appetite is glutted, and then the pleasure is over; and as soon as that is over, the general appetite of human nature for happiness returns, but empty, with nothing to satisfy it. So the glutting of a particular appetite only takes away from the general thirst of nature and leaves it empty. 4. Spiritual good is satisfying in that there is enough in it to satisfy the soul in degree, if only the obstacles were removed and the enjoying faculty duly applied. There is room enough here for the soul to stretch itself out; here is an infinite ocean of it. If men are not satisfied here in degree of happiness, the cause lies with themselves: it is because they do not open their mouths wide enough.

But these things do not show that a soul that has tasted a little has no appetite stirred up for more of the same, or that his appetite will not increase the more he tastes, until he comes to the fullness of enjoyment. So bodies that are drawn toward the globe of the earth tend toward it more strongly the nearer they come to the attracting body, and are not at rest outside its center. Spiritual good is of a satisfying nature; and for that very reason the soul that tastes it and knows its nature will thirst after it, and after a fullness of it, so that it may be satisfied. And the more he experiences and the more he knows this excellent, unparalleled, exquisite and satisfying sweetness, the more earnestly will he hunger and thirst for more, until he comes to perfection. And therefore it is the nature of spiritual affections that the greater they are, the greater the appetite and longing after grace and holiness.

But it is otherwise with joys and other religious affections that are false and counterfeit. If before there was a great desire of some sort after grace, then as these affections rise, #emph[that] desire ceases or is lessened. Perhaps before, while the man was under the convictions of the law and very afraid of hell, he earnestly longed to obtain spiritual light in his understanding, and faith in Christ, and love to God. But now that these false affections have risen, which deceive him and make him confident that he is converted and his state is good, there are no more earnest longings after light and grace. For his end has been met: he is confident that his sins are forgiven and that he will go to heaven, and so he is satisfied. And especially when false affections are raised very high, they put an end to longings after grace and holiness. The man is now far from seeing himself as a poor, empty creature. On the contrary, he is rich and has grown wealthy, and can hardly conceive of anything more excellent than what he has already attained.

That is why many persons’ earnestness in seeking comes to an end once they have obtained what they call their conversion, or at least once they have had those high affections that make them fully confident of it. Before, while they regarded themselves as in a state of nature, they were engaged in seeking after God and Christ, cried earnestly for grace, and strove in the use of means. But now they act as though they thought their work was done. They live on their first work, or on some high experiences that are past, and there is an end to their crying and striving after God and grace. #footnote[It is common to see a false heart most diligent in seeking the Lord when he has been at his worst, and most careless when he is at his best. That is why many at their first conversion sought the Lord earnestly, but afterward their affections and efforts die, so that now they think they are as good as the word can make them.—A hypocrite’s last end is to satisfy himself, so he has enough. A saint’s is to satisfy Christ, so he never has enough. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part I. P. 157. Many a man, perhaps, may say: I have nothing in myself, and all is in Christ; and comfort himself there, and so fall asleep. Hands off! Do not touch this ark, or the Lord will strike you dead. A Christ made of rags would serve your turn as well. Ibid p. 71. A hypocrite’s light goes out, and does not grow. That is why many who have stood a long time take all their comfort from their first work, and droop in old age. Ibid p. 77. And on p. 93, 94, Mr. #emph[Shepard,] describing those who have a dead hope, says: Those who content themselves with any measure of holiness and grace do not look for Christ’s coming and company. For saints who do look for him, though they do not have the holiness and grace they would like, still do not rest satisfied with any measure. 1 John 3:3: “Everyone who has this hope in Him purifies himself, just as Christ is pure.”—The saints are not content with any adornment until they are made glorious, and so fit for fellowship with that bridegroom.—When a man does not stop until he gets a certain measure of faith and grace, and now that he has got it, contents himself with it as a good sign that he will be saved, he is not looking for Christ. Or when men are heavily laden with sin, then embrace Christ, and then are comforted, sealed, and have joy that fills them; and now the work is done.—And when men will not content themselves with any measure, but wish they had more, if only grace would grow while they count the hours and sit idle; and so God must do it all, but they do not purge themselves and make a work of it. Again, p. 109: There is not a hypocrite living who does not come to Christ for his own ends, for he cannot work beyond his principle. Now when men have served their own turn out of another man, away they go, and keep what they have. A hypocrite comes to Christ as a man comes to a rich shop: he will not go to the cost of buying the whole shop, but only as much as serves his turn. Commonly men in horror seek as much of Christ as will ease them, and so they profess; and so they seek as much of Christ as will give them credit; and so their desires after Christ are soon satisfied. #emph[Appetitus Finis est infinitus.] \[The desire for the end is infinite.\] Woe to you who can paint such a Christ in your head, and receive such a Christ into your heart, as must be a pander to your sloth. The Lord will avenge this wrong done to his glory with greater sorrows than any ever felt: to make Christ not only food and drink to feed you, but clothes to cover your sloth.—Why, what can we do? What can we do?—Why, as the first #emph[Adam] passes on not only guilt but power, so the second passes on both righteousness and strength. Ibid p. 158. When the Lord has given some light and affection, and some comfort, and some reformation, now a man grows full here. Saints do things for God, and carnal hearts do something too; but a little fills them, and quiets them, and so damns them. That is why men are very diligent in the use of means at the first work on them; but after that they come to neglect prayer, sleep through sermons, and become careless, sapless and lifeless.— Ibid p. 210. It is evidence of a lack of grace when a man says to himself, as the glutton said to his soul, #emph[“You have plenty of good things laid up for many years. Take your rest.”] So you have repentance, and grace, and peace enough #emph[for many years;] and so the soul takes its rest, and grows sluggish and negligent. Oh, if you die in this condition, this night your soul will be taken away to hell. Ibid. P. 227.] But the holy principles that move a true saint have a far more powerful influence than slavish fear to stir him up to earnestness in seeking God and holiness. That is why seeking God is spoken of as one of the distinguishing marks of the saints, and #emph[those who seek God] is one of the names by which the godly are called in Scripture. Ps 24:6: “Such is the generation of those who seek Him, who seek Your face, O God of Jacob.” Ps 69:6: “May those who seek You not be dishonored through me.” Verse 32: “The humble will see and rejoice. You#super[+] who seek God, let your#super[+] hearts be revived!” And 70:4: “May all who seek You rejoice and be glad in You; may those who love Your salvation always say, ‘Let God be magnified!’ ” And the Scriptures everywhere represent the seeking, striving and labor of a Christian as coming chiefly after his conversion, and his conversion as only the beginning of his work. Almost everything said in the New Testament about men’s watching, paying earnest heed to themselves, running the race set before them, striving and agonizing, wrestling not against flesh and blood but against rulers and powers, fighting, putting on the whole armor of God and standing, having done everything, to stand, pressing forward, straining toward what is ahead, persisting in prayer, crying to God day and night: almost everything said in the New Testament about these things, I say, is spoken of the saints and directed to them. Where these things are applied once to sinners seeking conversion, they are spoken ten times of the saints carrying on the great business of their high calling. But many in these days have fallen into a strange way, contrary to Scripture, of having all their striving and wrestling over before they are converted, and so having an easy time of it afterward, sitting down to enjoy their sloth and idleness, as those whose wants are now supplied, and who have become rich and full. But when the Lord #emph[fills the hungry with good things, these rich] are likely to be #emph[sent away empty] (Luke 1:53).

But doubtless there are some hypocrites who have only false affections and who will think they can stand this test. They will readily say that they do not want to rest satisfied with past attainments, but to keep pressing forward: they do desire more, they long after God and Christ, and desire more holiness, and do seek it. But the truth is that their desires are not properly the desires of an appetite for holiness for its own sake, or for the moral excellence and holy sweetness that is in it, but only for ulterior ends. They long for clearer manifestations so that they may be better satisfied about the state of their souls. Or, because self is gratified in great manifestations, in being made so much of by God and so exalted above others, they long to taste the love of God (as they call it) more than to have more love to God. Or perhaps they have a kind of forced, imagined or manufactured longing, because they think they must long for more grace, or else it will be a dark sign against them. But things like these are far different from the natural, and as it were necessary, appetite and thirst of the new man after God and holiness. There is an inward burning desire after holiness in a saint, as natural to the new creature as vital heat is to the body. There is a holy breathing and panting after the Spirit of God to increase holiness, as natural to a holy nature as breathing is to a living body. And holiness or sanctification is more directly its object than any manifestation of God’s love and favor. This is the food and drink that is the object of the spiritual appetite. John 4:34: “My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me and to finish His work.” Where we read in Scripture of the desires, longings and thirstings of the saints, righteousness and God’s laws are much more often mentioned as their object than anything else. The saints #emph[crave pure spiritual milk,] not so much to testify God’s love to them as #emph[so that by it they may grow up] in holiness. I have shown before that holiness is the good which is the immediate object of a spiritual taste. But undoubtedly the same sweetness that is the chief object of a spiritual taste is also the chief object of a spiritual appetite. Grace is the godly man’s treasure. Isa 33:6: “The fear of the #smallcaps[Lord] is his treasure.” Godliness is the gain he is covetous and greedy for (1 Tim 6:6). Hypocrites long for manifestations more for the present comfort of the manifestation, and the high manifestation of God’s love in it, than for any sanctifying influence it has. But neither a longing after great manifestations or great tastes of the love of God, nor a longing to be in heaven, nor a longing to die, is in any measure so distinguishing a mark of true saints as longing after a more holy heart, and living a more holy life.

But I have now come to the last distinguishing mark of holy affections that I will mention.
